(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-medical-imaging-quiz"]={"title":"Principles of Medical Imaging Quiz 1 — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does the Hounsfield table refine the five basic radiographic densities?","choices":["It separates fat from air, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart","It merges bone and soft tissue, which a conventional radiograph shows separately","It separates water from soft tissue, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart","It separates calcium from metal, which a conventional radiograph cannot tell apart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck's asterisk on “Five*” marks exactly that exception. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Ordered from the most negative Hounsfield number to the most positive, what is the sequence of tissues?","choices":["Fat, air, water, bone, soft tissue, metal","Air, fat, water, soft tissue, bone, metal","Air, water, fat, soft tissue, bone, metal","Metal, bone, soft tissue, water, fat, air"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and it runs blackest to whitest in exactly that order. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which substance is assigned a Hounsfield number of zero by convention?","choices":["Air","Bone","Fat","Water"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — other tissues take a range of Hounsfield numbers according to their density. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the deck say about shellfish allergy and iodinated contrast?","choices":["There is cross-reactivity only with the iso-osmolal agents such as iodixanol","There should be no cross-reactivity between shellfish and iodinated radiocontrast","There is well-established cross-reactivity, so the two allergies must be treated as one","There is partial cross-reactivity, so contrast may be given at a reduced concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the cross-reactivity idea is explicitly rejected. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Who discovered x-rays, and what recognition followed?","choices":["Wilhelm Roentgen, in Germany in 1895, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901","Godfrey Hounsfield, in Britain in 1972, awarded the Nobel Prize for Medicine in 1979","Allan Cormack, in South Africa in 1963, awarded the first Nobel Prize for Physics","Marie Curie, in France in 1898, awarded the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1903"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the technique is still called roentgenography after him. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which phrase did Professor Reynolds call out as a buzzword, and what does it mean?","choices":["Coral-red fluorescence — erythrasma","Increased attenuation — a metallic foreign body","Free air under the diaphragm — perforated bowel","Air-fluid levels in dilated loops — small bowel obstruction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — unless there has been recent laparoscopic surgery and the insufflated gas is not yet absorbed. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:22:50"},{"q":"Which term describes tissue that appears white because it absorbs more of the beam?","choices":["Radiopaque, also called hyperdense or radiodense","Radiolucent, also called hypodense","Isodense, matching the density of the surrounding tissue","Attenuated, meaning the beam has been scattered away"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — less passage of x-ray photons produces the whiter appearance. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the window in computed tomography?","choices":["The interval during which the contrast sits at its peak concentration in the vessel","The slice thickness selected before the scan is acquired","The range of Hounsfield numbers pre-selected for display, spread over the available grey scale","The physical aperture of the gantry through which the patient passes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — for example only those densities between −100 and +300. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What must be checked before giving intravenous iodinated contrast, and why?","choices":["Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine, because the agent is nephrotoxic","Liver function tests and bilirubin, because the agent is cleared hepatically","Thyroid function tests, because the iodine load can precipitate thyrotoxicosis","Complete blood count and platelets, because the agent affects coagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and a litre of normal saline is given to protect the kidneys. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In the deck's table of typical organ radiation doses, which listed study delivers the highest dose?","choices":["Barium enema, to the colon","Adult abdominal computed tomography, to the stomach","Screening mammography, to the breast","Neonatal abdominal computed tomography, to the stomach"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — twenty, the largest figure in the table. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which tracer does positron emission tomography typically use, and why that one?","choices":["Gadolinium, a paramagnetic metal, because tumours disrupt the local magnetic environment","Fluorodeoxyglucose-18, a radioactive glucose, because tumours have a higher metabolic rate","Technetium-99, a metastable isotope, because tumours retain it far longer than normal tissue","Iodixanol, an iso-osmolal agent, because tumours take up iodine more avidly than muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the scan asks which tissues are consuming more glucose. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What does the colour Doppler setting add to a standard ultrasound image?","choices":["Depth of penetration and resolution, on an image otherwise limited to superficial planes","Density in Hounsfield units and windowing, on an image otherwise showing five densities","Metabolic uptake and intensity, on an image otherwise showing only anatomical structure","Direction of flow and velocity, on an image otherwise made of white, grey and black"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — colour is the flow overlay, and blood is what it is used for. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What are the midsagittal and parasagittal planes?","choices":["Midsagittal runs through the umbilicus; parasagittal sections lie above and below it","Midsagittal runs across the shoulders; parasagittal sections lie anterior and posterior","Midsagittal runs at the level of the heart; parasagittal sections are angled off the axis","Midsagittal runs in the body's midline; parasagittal sections lie to either side of it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the midsagittal plane is also called the median plane. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which disadvantages are named for conventional radiography?","choices":["Operator dependence and an inability to penetrate bone or large gas-filled structures","Long scan times and safety problems with magnetic implanted devices","Very high radiation dose and an inability to be made portable at all","A limited range of densities and reliance on ionizing radiation, though at relatively low dose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the dose is low compared with other radiographic modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does single photon emission computed tomography measure, and what does it show?","choices":["Attenuated x-rays on a rotating detector ring, giving cross-sectional images by density","Single photons on rotating gamma cameras, giving three-dimensional images of where blood flows","Paired positrons on a stationary gamma camera, giving two-dimensional images of glucose uptake","Reflected sound on a hand-held transducer, giving real-time images of moving structures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck's stated indication is seeing where blood flows to. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which advantages are named for computed tomography?","choices":["It is superior to magnetic resonance imaging for differentiating soft tissues","It expands the grey scale beyond the five basic densities, reduces overlapping of structures, works with implantable devices, and allows three-dimensional reconstruction","It uses no ionizing radiation at all and is described as being the safest of all of the imaging modalities that are currently available anywhere","It is fully portable, inexpensive, and can be brought to the patient's bedside"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is described as the cornerstone of cross-sectional imaging. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the deck call the most important thing to remember about ultrasound orientation?","choices":["Which frequency is selected: high for superficial structures, low for deeper structures","Where the indicator is: on the right of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the left for everything else","Where the indicator is: on the left of the screen in cardiac imaging, on the right for everything else","How deep the focus is set: shallow for superficial structures, deep for abdominal structures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is called crucial, because the whole image flips with it. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Why can magnetic resonance imaging show tissues that are surrounded by bone better than computed tomography can?","choices":["Bone is windowed out during acquisition, so only the soft tissue range is displayed","Bone absorbs the radio waves, so the surrounding soft tissue returns a relatively brighter signal","Calcium emits no signal, so the bone does not obscure what sits inside it","Calcium emits a strong signal, so the bone can be subtracted from the image afterwards"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — silence from calcium is an advantage rather than a gap. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Professor Reynolds said renal function must be checked before gadolinium, but for a different reason from iodinated contrast. What is that reason?","choices":["Gadolinium is far more nephrotoxic than iodinated contrast, so the threshold for withholding it is lower","Gadolinium is cleared hepatically, so renal function predicts the hepatic reserve","Gadolinium interferes with the creatinine assay, so a baseline is needed for comparison","It is a clearance problem — poor function lets it build up in tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — gadolinium is not necessarily nephrotoxic itself, but if the kidney cannot clear it, it accumulates in tissue and that accumulation is toxic. With iodinated contrast the agent itself is nephrotoxic. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:40:24"},{"q":"What method did Professor Reynolds give for choosing a modality when you are not certain of the diagnosis?","choices":["Always order both a plain film and a cross-sectional study at the same visit","Memorise a protocol for each presenting complaint and follow it exactly","Reason from the tissue rather than from a memorised protocol","Always begin with the study carrying the least radiation and escalate only if negative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bone points to x-ray or computed tomography; soft tissue often starts with ultrasound. Memorised protocols are fine, but reasoning from the tissue is what generalises. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:10:31"},{"q":"Which enteric pathologies is the abdominal series named as evaluating?","choices":["Small bowel obstruction, perforation and volvulus","Appendicitis, diverticulitis and inflammatory bowel disease","Cholelithiasis, pancreatitis and hepatic abscess","Peptic ulceration, gastritis and oesophageal stricture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the three named examples of enteric pathology. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What determines the frequency of the radio waves a magnetic resonance scanner reads?","choices":["The chemical environment of the hydrogen atoms, together with their location","The strength of the magnet in Tesla, together with the duration of the scan","The density of the tissue in Hounsfield units, together with the chosen window","The metabolic rate of the tissue, together with the dose of tracer given"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both, which is what lets the computer build a spatial map. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which density is described as the most dense naturally occurring material?","choices":["Calcium","Metal","Fluid","Fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it absorbs most x-rays, though metal absorbs essentially all of them. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which vascular magnetic resonance studies are performed without contrast?","choices":["Coronary angiography and retrograde pyelography","Arthrography and intrathecal myelography","Magnetic resonance angiography and magnetic resonance venography","Computed tomography angiography and computed tomography venography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both are done without a contrast agent. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"In which patients is ultrasound named as often the first study of choice?","choices":["Female pelvis and paediatric patients, and it is also used to guide procedures","Adult chest and geriatric patients, and it is also used to screen for fractures","Head-injured and intoxicated patients, and it is also used to exclude a bleed","Oncology and post-operative patients, and it is also used to stage known disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — no radiation is the reason it leads in both groups. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does an abdominal series evaluate, and how is the patient positioned?","choices":["The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient standing, to assess air-fluid levels or free air","The genitourinary tract, with the patient supine, to assess stones within the collecting system","The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient supine, to assess bowel wall thickness and oedema","The retroperitoneum, with the patient in decubitus, to assess the psoas shadows and the flanks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — upright is what makes free air and air-fluid levels visible. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is given alongside intravenous iodinated contrast to protect the kidneys?","choices":["One dose of intravenous furosemide","One litre of normal saline","One litre of lactated Ringer's","One gram of acetylcysteine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the only volume the deck specifies, and it is renal protection. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What practical ordering point does the deck make about imaging several body regions?","choices":["Multiple body parts must be imaged on separate days to limit cumulative radiation dose","Multiple body parts or sections may mean multiple orders, giving brain, cervical, thoracic and lumbar spine as the example","Multiple body parts should be combined into one single order, so that the patient is scanned once and the radiologist reads the whole study together","Multiple body parts require the radiologist to countersign the request before booking"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — one clinical question can require four separate requests. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How does a computed tomography scanner acquire its data?","choices":["A gamma camera mounted on a rotating gantry detects the photons that are emitted from an injected isotope","High-frequency sound waves released from a transducer bounce off tissue and return","A varying magnetic field aligns hydrogen atoms which then emit radio waves","Powerful x-ray beams pass through the patient via a rotating fan beam, measuring transmission at thousands of data points"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a computer then processes the data through algorithms into diagnostic images. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How does the lung dose of a lateral chest radiograph compare with the posterior-anterior view?","choices":["It is identical, because both views expose the same volume of lung tissue","It is higher, but only in children, because the chest wall is thinner","It is higher, which is one reason the posterior-anterior view is the preferred projection","It is lower, which is one reason the lateral view is taken first in a standard series"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the deck also credits the posterior-anterior view with lower dose to sensitive organs. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
